

OOP terms:

class is a blueprint that describes the data structure and methods/functionality of an object

object is a specific instance created from a class 

methods defines what an object can do, which was instantiated from a class

constructors are special class member functions that are used for initializing data of a class

encapsulation is the practice of hiding internal class data and only allowing access to these via public methods like getters and setters

abstraction is the concept of hiding complex implementation to make a simple interface 

composition is when a class has instances of other classes as attributes
for example: we have a class called Point that has an object (x,y), and a city class that contains an object called location that is an instance of Point actually, and through this its possible to store a point (x,y) in location as an attribute

inheritance is when a child/class inherits attirbutes and methods of a base/parent class.

polymorphism is the concept of treating an object of a specific type as various types based on its inheritance hierarchy


Systems Engineering is a method of how we can develop complex projects that meet objectives in an efficient way.
    efficiency by minimizing rework catching errors early, and communicating effectively

Whats systems engineering and why is it useful

Systems Engineering is a process that we can use to build and design a complex idea
    that could be anything from a complex infrastructure, a plane, or a fridge.
    - and its all about guiding the engineering process so taht the system can be implemented and meet the needs of a project
        - that are according to stakeholders, project maangers, and other engineers
    - and typically this process involves a lot of review cycles using tools that allow simulation and analysis that assess and update the system.

    - why systems engineering as an approach? 
        - Its the recommended approach involving projects that are too copmlex to build and design as a single object. It allows something to be built as multiple, functional pieces that make up a system, which are easier to develop and manage.

for example, we need to build a personal taxi service
    - we have to figure out what does it need to do?
        - it needs to be able to have a low cost per mile for a person
    - we have figure out potential concepts
        - should it be designed as a truck, sports car, or sedan
    - we have to figure out the performance requirements
        - can it drive on public roads, how often does it need maintenace, is it environmentally safe?
    - then we need to look at how each component of the system works together
        - the software, the chassis, and the engine
    - then we we go through simulation and analysis  to update the system and then desing and manufacture and build and verify the system through integration and testing



